1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,420
Proposition forty-four sets a spending rule for private nonprofit safety-net

2
00:00:04,420 --> 00:00:09,440
clinics. These clinics serve many low-income and uninsured patients. The

3
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:14,480
proposal does not apply the same rule to every doctor, hospital, or publicly

4
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:18,860
operated clinic. Covered clinics would have to spend at least ninety percent

5
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:23,360
of annual revenue on health care services, leaving no more than ten percent

6
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,820
for other expenses. The attorney general would define the categories in more

7
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:28,340
detail.

8
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:33,113
Imagine a clinic manager planning next year’s budget. For a hypothetical

9
00:00:33,113 --> 00:00:38,033
clinic receiving ten million dollars, the ninety percent minimum is nine

10
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:43,013
million dollars. If qualifying spending is eight million, the shortfall is

11
00:00:43,013 --> 00:00:48,193
one million. This is arithmetic to explain the rule, not a real clinic’s

12
00:00:48,193 --> 00:00:52,533
budget. A nurse depends on both care resources and working systems.

13
00:00:53,133 --> 00:00:57,553
A clinic falling short would owe a penalty equal to the shortfall. It could

14
00:00:57,553 --> 00:01:02,213
recover that money by complying within five years. Temporary waivers would

15
00:01:02,213 --> 00:01:06,413
be available in some circumstances. The rule therefore has enforcement

16
00:01:06,413 --> 00:01:11,333
mechanisms as well as a target. Which costs qualify matters. An

17
00:01:11,333 --> 00:01:16,013
administrative label is not proof that an expense is wasteful; scheduling,

18
00:01:16,013 --> 00:01:20,653
records, and other systems can support care. Final definitions affect how

19
00:01:20,653 --> 00:01:25,173
clinics would change their budgets. The analyst estimates enforcement costs

20
00:01:25,173 --> 00:01:30,133
in the low tens of millions of dollars each year, funded by fees on affected

21
00:01:30,133 --> 00:01:36,053
clinics. Other public costs are uncertain and depend on how clinics respond,

22
00:01:36,053 --> 00:01:38,913
including whether some reduce operations or close.

23
00:01:39,567 --> 00:01:44,287
A voter might want a larger share of clinic revenue devoted to services for

24
00:01:44,287 --> 00:01:49,087
patients, with reporting and consequences for falling short. They may

25
00:01:49,087 --> 00:01:54,267
believe a clear minimum is a useful accountability tool. Reaching a ratio,

26
00:01:54,267 --> 00:01:58,307
however, does not by itself measure quality or outcomes.

27
00:01:58,933 --> 00:02:03,253
You might ask whether one ratio fits different clinics. Another voter could

28
00:02:03,253 --> 00:02:08,633
worry that a rigid limit constrains necessary support costs, adds fees, or

29
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:13,133
makes some clinics financially fragile. A clinic can improve its reported

30
00:02:13,133 --> 00:02:18,593
ratio in different ways; the important outcome is access to effective care,

31
00:02:18,593 --> 00:02:20,493
not only the percentage on a report.

32
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:26,820
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 34 percent said yes, 61

33
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:32,860
percent no, and 6 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not

34
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:33,960
votes already cast.

35
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:39,440
Yes adopts the new spending rule; no leaves existing reporting and spending

36
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:44,340
requirements in place. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source

37
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:49,200
links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own

38
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:50,240
informed choice.
